       sgitopnm - convert a SGI image file to PNM


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ssggiittooppnnmm

       [--vveerrbboossee]

       [--cchhaannnneell _c]

       [_S_g_i_F_i_l_e_N_a_m_e]



DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ssggiittooppnnmm  reads an SGI image file as input and produces a PGM image for
       a 2-dimensional (1- or 2-channel) input file, and a  PPM  image  for  a
       3-dimensional (3 or more channels) input file.

       Alternatively, the program produces a PGM image of any one of the chan‐
       nels in the input file.

       Before Netpbm 10.67 (June 2014), ssggiittooppnnmm does not  work  on  2-channel
       SGI images.  It fails if you try.

       If  you  don't specify the _S_g_i_F_i_l_e_N_a_m_e argument, input is from Standard
       Input.

       Before Netpbm 10.67 (June 2014), ssggiittooppnnmm requires its input  to  be  a
       seekable file, so for example you can't feed it from a pipe.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ssggiittooppnnmm  recognizes  the
       following command line options:



       --vveerrbboossee
              Give some information about the SGI image file.


       --cchhaannnneell _n
              Extract channel _n of the image as a PGM image.

              Without this option, ssggiittooppnnmm extracts the first 3 channels as a
              PPM image or, if the input has 1 or  2  channels,  extracts  the
              first channel as a PGM image.

              A 2-channel image is grayscale plus transparency, so you can get
              the transparency information with --cchhaannnneell==22.   You  could  then
              combine them into a PAM image of tuple type GRAYSCALE_ALPHA with
              ppaammssttaacckk.




RREEFFEERREENNCCEESS
       The   SGI   image   format   specification   version    1.0    is    at
       ftp://ftp.sgi.com/graphics/grafica/sgiimage.html .

       There  is  an example SGI file at https://github.com/ZaaLabs/ZaaIL-Tes‐
       tImages/tree/master/SGI .



